(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-mi-acs-quiz"]={"title":"Acute Coronary Syndrome & Fibrinolytics Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What activates and aggregates platelets once collagen is exposed at the injury?","choices":["Tissue factor and factor VIIa","Fibrinogen and plasminogen","Prothrombin and factor X","Adenosine diphosphate and thromboxane A2"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine diphosphate and thromboxane A2 activate and aggregate platelets at the injured plaque surface. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which vascular condition contraindicates fibrinolytics?","choices":["Peripheral artery disease","Varicose veins","Raynaud phenomenon","Aortic dissection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aortic dissection is a listed contraindication, since fibrinolytics could worsen bleeding from the torn aortic wall. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which fibrinolytic is contraindicated in a patient who received it before or had an allergic reaction to it?","choices":["Alteplase","Reteplase","Tenecteplase","Streptokinase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prior exposure to streptokinase or an allergic reaction to it is a listed contraindication to using it again. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What advantage do reteplase and tenecteplase have over alteplase?","choices":["No risk of intracranial hemorrhage","A longer half-life","No risk of allergic reactions","Activation of plasminogen without fibrin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Reteplase and tenecteplase have a longer half-life than tissue plasminogen activator (alteplase). — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"When do fibrinolytics give the most benefit in ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["Late, after a day of symptoms","Only after stent placement","Early after symptom onset","After cardiac biomarkers normalize"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Benefit is greatest when fibrinolytics are given early after symptom onset, and there is less benefit with later presentation. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which finding contraindicates fibrinolytics?","choices":["Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Previous myocardial infarction","Active bleeding","Hypercholesterolemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Active bleeding or a hemorrhagic disorder contraindicates fibrinolytics, which cause bleeding as their main adverse effect. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which findings should be checked before and after a beta blocker is given in acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Heart rate and blood pressure","Body temperature and weight","Serum sodium and calcium","Urine output and appetite"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cautions are bradycardia and hypotension, so heart rate and blood pressure are watched closely. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which step begins thrombus formation over an atherosclerotic plaque?","choices":["Platelet aggregation at the plaque","Formation of fibrin strands","Injury to the overlying endothelium","Occlusion of the lumen by thrombus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Spontaneous plaque rupture or procedural injury such as balloon deployment leaves injured endothelium, which starts platelet adhesion and the clotting cascade. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which set of adverse reactions is expected with nitrates?","choices":["Hypotension, headache, reflex tachycardia","Hypertension, headache, reflex bradycardia","Hypotension, cough, reflex bradycardia","Hypotension, constipation, reflex tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nitrate vasodilation causes hypotension and headache, and the pressure fall can trigger reflex tachycardia. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which patient factor contraindicates fibrinolytics?","choices":["Pregnancy","Hyperlipidemia","Obesity","Smoking history"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pregnancy is a listed contraindication to fibrinolytics, because of bleeding risk. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"How does streptokinase activate plasminogen?","choices":["It binds plasminogen already attached to fibrin","It forms a one-to-one complex with it","It is itself converted to plasmin","It blocks plasminogen activator inhibitor-1"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Streptokinase forms a stable one-to-one complex with plasminogen that exposes a catalytic site and converts plasminogen to plasmin. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which drug should a patient chew and swallow at the first signs of chest pain from suspected acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Clopidogrel","Nitroglycerin","Aspirin","Metoprolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is used at the first signs of chest pain and reduces mortality and reinfarction in both ST-elevation and non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Why are nitrates used in acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["To lower mortality","To relieve chest pain","To limit infarct size","To prevent reinfarction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nitrates give relief of chest pain only; they show no mortality benefit and no improvement in outcomes. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"An electrocardiogram shows ST-segment elevation in a patient with ischemic chest discomfort. What is the working diagnosis?","choices":["ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Unstable angina","Non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Stable angina"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. ST-segment elevation with ischemic discomfort defines ST-elevation myocardial infarction, the form for which fibrinolytics are indicated. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which fibrinolytic is chiefly associated with fever, chills and rash?","choices":["Streptokinase","Alteplase","Reteplase","Tenecteplase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fever, chills and skin rash occur mainly with streptokinase; allergic reactions, including anaphylaxis, can occur with any fibrinolytic. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"For which condition are fibrinolytics indicated?","choices":["Unstable angina","Non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Stable angina","ST-elevation myocardial infarction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fibrinolytics are indicated in ST-elevation myocardial infarction, where they restore coronary flow by lysing the clot. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which factor starts the plasma clotting cascade at the site of vessel injury?","choices":["Thromboxane A2","Tissue factor","Adenosine diphosphate","Plasminogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tissue factor, with factor VIIa, starts the plasma clotting cascade at the injury, leading to prothrombin becoming thrombin and then fibrin. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which outcomes does aspirin reduce in acute coronary syndrome?","choices":["Resting heart rate and contractility","Preload through venous dilation","Reinfarction, stroke and cardiac death","Clot size through direct lysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin decreases recurrent ischemia, reinfarction, stroke and cardiac death. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which drug is withheld from a patient with suspected acute coronary syndrome who has an active gastrointestinal bleed?","choices":["Metoprolol","Nitroglycerin","Morphine","Aspirin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A gastrointestinal bleed is a contraindication to aspirin, along with allergy and recent intracranial hemorrhage, because aspirin raises bleeding risk. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Why is morphine controversial in unstable angina and non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction?","choices":["It may raise mortality","It fails to relieve ischemic pain","It markedly increases bleeding risk","It cannot be combined with aspirin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Morphine may have increased mortality in unstable angina and non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction, so its use there is controversial. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A patient with acute coronary syndrome has severe wheezing from reactive airway disease. Which drug class calls for caution?","choices":["Beta blockers","Nitrates","Heparins","Fibrinolytics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Severe reactive airway disease is a listed caution for beta blockers in acute coronary syndrome. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which drug group is used in conjunction with fibrinolytic or antiplatelet therapy to block the clotting cascade?","choices":["Beta blockers","Nitrates","Heparins","Morphine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heparins are anticoagulants used in conjunction with fibrinolysis or antiplatelet agents. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What happens to an infarct in the hours after coronary occlusion if blood flow is not restored?","choices":["It stays the same size","It grows into ischemic tissue","It shrinks without treatment","It stays limited to its core"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The infarct enlarges over hours and grows into the area of ischemia, which is why restoring coronary blood flow is a goal of therapy. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which active enzyme lyses the fibrin in a clot?","choices":["Plasminogen","Thrombin","Plasminogen activator inhibitor-1","Plasmin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Plasmin is formed from plasminogen and lyses fibrin. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is shared by all fibrinolytic agents?","choices":["Pulmonary fibrosis","Bleeding","Hypoglycemia","Constipation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bleeding, including intracranial hemorrhage, is the adverse effect of all fibrinolytics. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 62"}]};
